{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793625449,"nonce":"GZvrqexXFNiat6XtnI03krmvbiw=","message":"Optimizer pass skipping, tried and not kept (2026-11-02). Rule: a pass that found nothing to do is skipped until something changes the module (an epoch bumped by each pass's reported change or by a move of the module's shape: function value count, block and instruction counts); the round loop wraps seventeen passes in it. On Lua method_call's entry chunk (four rounds) it skips nothing: round 2's last pass, cfg_simplify, merges blocks, which bumps the epoch after every other pass has run, so the confirming round starts from a changed module and runs all seventeen (13.7 ms of passes with or without the rule). The confirming round costs about 12% of the chunk's pass time and cannot be cut by this granularity; a per-pass dependency table (cfg_simplify's merges are what let scalar_replace_alloc, licm and load_cse find more) would be needed and is the kind of rule that quietly costs code quality. Also measured on that chunk (body of 412 blocks, 1831 instruction lines): the 108 error and return blocks (zl_index_nil 61, raise_call_nil 20, annotate 42) are 21% of the instruction lines and fall into 8 distinct shapes once value ids are normalised (30 identical copies of the most common); a tail-merge of cold exits that differ only in constants (line, description) into one block with phis would cut roughly 15% of the chunk's instructions and so of its optimize and Cranelift time. Not started.","files":null}]}